 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Earth just had its hottest October ever measured. Is a record year next?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Europe's Copernicus climate service says October averaged one point seven five degrees above pre-industrial levels. That makes it the warmest October in its records. Its climate lead called the warmth remarkable."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The U.S. agency Noah agrees. On Thursday, it said October was the warmest since its records began in eighteen fifty. Noah says ocean temperatures set a monthly record for the fifth month in a row. It also says an El Niño is still under way in the Pacific."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "So, is a record year next? Copernicus says twenty twenty-six is virtually certain to end above one point five degrees. But it most likely ranks second, behind twenty twenty-four. It puts the chance of a tie or a new record at about one in five."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will twenty twenty-six beat twenty twenty-four as the hottest year? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
